A curvature-continuous (G2) fillet section: ChFi3d_CurvatureContinuous.

What it adds
  A fourth ChFi3d_FilletShape. The fillet runs between the same contact
  lines as the rolling ball of the given radius, so everything upstream of
  the section (the walking, the extremities, the corners and the topology)
  is untouched; only the cross-section changes. Where the circle meets each
  face with curvature 1/R against the face's own, the new section is the
  quintic Bezier that leaves each contact point along the circle's tangent
  with the face's normal curvature in that direction, so the blend is G2
  to its faces (Pegna & Wolter's linkage curve theorem: two surfaces
  tangent along a curve are curvature continuous when their normal
  curvatures agree in one transverse direction). The section passes
  through the circle's midpoint, so it adds or removes the depth the
  circular fillet would; its control polygon is convex for every dihedral
  angle below pi, so it has no inflection.

  BlendFunc_ConstRad builds the section (BlendFunc_CurvatureContinuous) and
  its first and second derivatives along the spine as central differences
  of the pole function at the contact data carried to first, respectively
  second, order with the derivatives it already has exactly, which makes
  both differences exact to O(h^2) with h a thousandth of the radius. The
  shape travels on ChFiDS_FilSpine, where ChFi3d_KParticular reads it to
  keep the analytic cylinder and torus shortcuts, which are circular in
  section, for the circular shapes. A contour this section cannot be
  built on, a variable radius, a blend that follows a face boundary or a
  circular simulation, is marked ChFiDS_SectionNotApplicable and
  abandoned rather than given a circle it did not ask for.

How it was measured
  On every edge a blend shares with the image of a face its edge lay on
  (the rails), sampled 33 times, the normal angle and the jump in normal
  curvature between the blend and the face, both faces' second derivative
  taken along one transverse direction against one reference normal:
  - box 40 x 30 x 10, the four vertical edges, R = 4: valid, no shell
    fault, no gap; rails G1 to 0 deg, curvature jump 1.0e-16 /mm where the
    circular fillet's is 0.25 /mm (= 1/R). Volume 11867.37 against the
    circular 11862.65 mm^3.
  - the same box, R = 4.9 (the two blends 0.2 mm apart): valid, 8.3e-16.
  - the box's four top edges (two-edge corners), R = 2: valid, 2.9e-15.
  - a dia. 12 boss on a 40 x 40 x 4 plate, its concave root circle, R = 4
    and R = 2: valid, 9.3e-16 and 5.6e-15 /mm, 1.5e-4 deg at the cylinder.
  - its convex rim, and root and rim together, R = 2: valid, 1.8e-13.
  - a dia. 12 bar half buried along the plate, its two straight root
    generators, R = 2, where the matched curvature is 1/6 on the cylinder
    side and 0 on the plate: valid, 4.8e-15 /mm.
  - all twelve box edges, R = 2 (three-edge corners): valid solid, rails
    2.1e-15 /mm, but the generic corner patch meets the blends at 2.78 deg
    and a curvature jump of 12.9 /mm where the circular case's spherical
    patch meets its cylinders at 8.5e-7 deg. The caller must refuse a
    vertex where three such edges meet; OCCT's corner construction is not
    changed here.
  The circular shapes are unchanged: the harness read 1/R at every rail of
  every control, and the same volumes, faces and validity as before.

Upstream status
  Not yet offered upstream. It is additive (a new enum value in three
  enums, a member on ChFiDS_FilSpine, a gate in ChFi3d_KParticular, the
  section itself) and would be a candidate once the three-edge corner has
  a G2 construction of its own.

